If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and requests an opioid-dosage-reduction or requests an opioid-discontinuation then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and has a pain that improves and that can possibly indicate an underlying-cause-resolution then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and has a pain that the long-term-opioid-therapy does not meaningfully reduce and has a function that the long-term-opioid-therapy does not meaningfully improve then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy for a prolonged-period and has an unclear-benefit-risk-balance then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and receives a higher-opioid-dosage and does not provably have a higher-dosage-benefit-evidence then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and experiences a side-effect that diminishes a quality-of-life or that impairs a function then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and has an opioid-misuse-evidence then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and experiences an overdose then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and experiences an other-serious-event then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and has an impending-event-warning-sign then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and receives an adverse-outcome-risk-increasing-medication then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
If a patient receives a long-term-opioid-therapy and has an adverse-outcome-risk-increasing-medical-condition then every clinician should consider a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper for the patient and should consider an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation for the patient and should discuss a reduced-opioid-dosage-taper with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation and should discuss an opioid-taper-and-discontinuation with the patient before a dosage-change-initiation.
A prolonged-period-example is a multi-year-period-example.
An unclear-benefit-risk-balance-example includes a tolerance-related-positive-effect-decrease-example and includes a possibly-opioid-related-reduced-focus-or-memory-symptom-example.
An other-serious-event-example is a hospitalization-or-injury-causing-event-example.
An impending-event-warning-sign-example includes a confusion-example and includes a sedation-example and includes a slurred-speech-example.
An adverse-outcome-risk-increasing-medication-example is a benzodiazepine-example.
An adverse-outcome-risk-increasing-medical-condition-example includes a sleep-apnea-example and includes a liver-disease-example and includes a kidney-disease-example and includes a fall-risk-example.
